FINAL ANSWER:
The code is concentrated in one person, but not entirely. Fatih Arslan holds 59.5% of the live code. The next person holds 7.6%.

**Who holds the live lines** (`git blame -w -M -C` at HEAD, 1,460 non-blank lines, 1,832 with blank lines):

| Author | Lines | Share | Share with blank lines | Commits | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Fatih Arslan | 869 | 59.5% | 61.7% | 80 (57.6%) | 2026-03-20 |
| Linda_pp | 111 | 7.6% | 7.5% | 1 | 2017-05-23 |
| Matthew Hall | 84 | 5.8% | 5.4% | 1 | 2026-03-04 |
| Greg Poirson | 49 | 3.4% | 3.5% | 7 | 2023-10-17 |
| Alex Guerrieri | 46 | 3.2% | 2.6% | 1 | 2015-10-26 |
| Albert | 44 | 3.0% | 2.9% | 3 | 2016-10-19 |
| Robert Pająk | 41 | 2.8% | 2.3% | 4 | 2023-03-01 |
| Cameron Moore | 40 | 2.7% | 2.7% | 1 | 2016-09-27 |
| Andrew Austin | 37 | 2.5% | 2.6% | 1 | 2015-08-23 |
| Klaus Post | 28 | 1.9% | 1.9% | 1 | 2024-04-08 |
| majiayu000 | 24 | 1.6% | 1.5% | 1 | 2026-01-01 |
| Thimo | 15 | 1.0% | 0.8% | 1 | 2014-11-26 |

32 more authors hold 4.9% together. The largest of them is Martin Lindhe at 0.9%.

**Bus factor**
- The line-based bus factor is 1. This is the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines reach 50%, computed over the whole repository.
- Reaching 80% takes 6 authors (5 when blank lines are counted).
- These figures measure how concentrated the surviving code is. They do not measure who understands it.

**Assessment**
- It is not a one-person project. One maintainer owns a clear majority and is still active, with the last commit on 2026-03-20.
- Past that, ownership is spread thinly across many contributors. Most of them made one commit and have not committed in years.
- Two recent contributors are more relevant for review than the old ones. Matthew Hall (5.8%) committed on 2026-03-04 and majiayu000 (1.6%) on 2026-01-01.
- If Fatih Arslan left, nobody else holds more than 7.6% of the code.

**What was left out or adjusted**
- **Dropped files:** 4 files and 237 lines are not counted. These are the vendored code (128 lines), the generated `attribute_string.go` (103 lines) and `go.sum` (6 lines).
- **Bots:** `dependabot[bot]` is excluded (7 lines, 38 commits). Its lines are not credited to anyone.
- **Reformat commit:** `4831a13` (`interface{}` → `any`) is listed in the repo's `.git-blame-ignore-revs`, so its lines go to the earlier authors.
- **Mechanical rewrites:** I checked 97 commits and set none aside. One more looks mechanical and was kept: `52aa0a6` (typo fix by Leo Correa, 1 surviving line), which is too small to matter.
- **Copy detection:** The figures use `-w -M -C`. Without `-C`, Fatih Arslan drops to 56.6% (827 lines), and Alex Guerrieri to 1.6% (24 lines). Raj Patel would rise from 0.2% (3 lines) to 6.0% (88 lines). A copy of 85 lines in one file is credited to other authors under `-C`. Even without `-C`, Fatih Arslan is still the top author.
- **Gap check:** Linda_pp and Matthew Hall have line shares more than 5 times their commit shares. I checked their commits. Both are real feature or fix work, not mass rewrites. These are `570b54c` (Hi* color helpers, 144 lines added) and `cbda2c3` (escape-code byte-count fix, 98 lines added).
- **Duplicate names:** Greg Poirson also appears as "greg" and Albert also appears as "bogem". Each pair is one address and is already counted as one person.
- **Possible same person (weak evidence):** Erik Hollensbe may appear under `<email redacted>` and `github@q33us`. The table counts these separately. Merged, he would hold 9 lines (0.6%). That is a guess, and it does not change the picture.

**What the numbers cannot show:** line counts say nothing about code quality, review work, design discussions or code that was deleted on purpose. This is a measure of who wrote the surviving lines, not of who is best placed to maintain them.

